---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Motif from "@hara-lang/visual-language/astro/Motif.astro";
const animationCode = `(defn scene [frame]
  (-> frame
      (character :pilot)
      (move-to [420 180] :ease-out)
      (speak "The program is the storyboard.")))

(play! (timeline (scene 0) (scene 48)))`;
const serviceCode = `(defroute GET "/orders/:id" [{:keys [id]}]
  (let [order (orders/find id)]
    (respond 200
      {:order order
       :total (orders/total order)})))`;
const ledgerCode = `(defn transfer [chain from to amount]
  (-> chain
      (assert-balance from amount)
      (debit from amount)
      (credit to amount)
      (commit {:kind :transfer})))`;
---
<SiteLayout title="Hara — one embeddable language, many runtimes">
  <main>
    <Motif kind="edge" class="hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="hara-kicker">One language · three deployment modes</p>
        <h1>One language.<br /><span>Fit to its environment.</span></h1>
        <p>Use Hara on the JVM, embed the native Rust engine, or assemble a browser kernel from only the capabilities the application needs.</p>
        <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/install.sh">Get Hara</a><a class="button" href="https://playground.hara-lang.org/">Open playground</a></div>
        <small>Measured artifacts, checksums, and methods are published with every benchmark.</small>
      </div>
    </Motif>

    <section class="proof" aria-label="Hara capabilities">
      <div><strong>Java</strong><span>JVM · Truffle · Native Image</span></div>
      <div><strong>Native</strong><span>Rust full compilation</span></div>
      <div><strong>Web</strong><span>Modular core → full</span></div>
      <div><strong>One HAL</strong><span>Keep the language and values</span></div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section intro">
      <p class="hara-kicker">The advantage</p>
      <h2>The language goes where the data lives.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Embed Hara and grant a program only the operations it needs. The runtime stays small, the data stays close, and authority remains visible.</p>
      <div class="card-grid three">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Fast kernel</h3><p>Prepared bytecode and whole-program compilation for native, browser, JVM, and Native Image hosts.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Durable values</h3><p>Persistent maps, vectors, sets, queues, sorted collections, tries, and deterministic hashing.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Controlled interop</h3><p>Files, sockets, processes, extensions, and host objects are explicit capabilities.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <p class="hara-kicker">Choose the environment</p>
      <h2>One kernel model, fitted three ways.</h2>
      <div class="kernel-picker">
        <label for="kernel-mode">Kernel mode</label>
        <select id="kernel-mode"><option value="java">Java</option><option value="native">Native</option><option value="web">Web</option></select>
        <div class="kernel-spec" data-kernel-mode="java"><code>Java</code><h3>JVM development with Truffle optimization.</h3><p>Run as <b>hara-jvm-full</b>, then compile the application to a GraalVM Native Image for a self-contained deployment.</p><dl><div><dt>Engine</dt><dd>Truffle AST / HBC4</dd></div><div><dt>Deployment</dt><dd>JVM or Native Image</dd></div><div><dt>Best for</dt><dd>Java systems and language tooling</dd></div></dl></div>
        <div class="kernel-spec" data-kernel-mode="native" hidden><code>Native</code><h3>The primary high-performance Rust runtime.</h3><p><b>hara-rust-full</b> prepares whole-function WebAssembly and hosts it natively. The VM tier remains available for controlled internal comparisons.</p><dl><div><dt>Engine</dt><dd>Whole-Wasm</dd></div><div><dt>Deployment</dt><dd>Native binary or library</dd></div><div><dt>Best for</dt><dd>Services, CLIs and embedded products</dd></div></dl></div>
        <div class="kernel-spec" data-kernel-mode="web" hidden><code>Web</code><h3>Start with core; load power only when required.</h3><p><b>hara-wasm-core</b> provides interpreter sessions and every browser bridge. VM and trace load as isolated HTA providers; <b>hara-wasm-full</b> is the all-in compilation tier.</p><dl><div><dt>Small</dt><dd>Interpreter core</dd></div><div><dt>Full</dt><dd>VM + whole-Wasm compiler</dd></div><div><dt>Best for</dt><dd>Docs, tools and browser applications</dd></div></dl></div>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/benchmarks/">Compare every artifact →</a>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section code-showcase">
      <p class="hara-kicker">Expressive by construction</p>
      <h2>Complex applications should read like their intent.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Hara keeps data, behavior, and host authority visible. The result is compact code you can enjoy reading—and confidently change while the system is alive.</p>
      <div class="code-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Hara application examples">
        <button class="active" data-example="animation">Animation</button><button data-example="service">Service</button><button data-example="ledger">Ledger</button>
      </div>
      <div class="code-stage">
        <pre data-code="animation"><code>{animationCode}</code></pre>
        <pre data-code="service" hidden><code>{serviceCode}</code></pre>
        <pre data-code="ledger" hidden><code>{ledgerCode}</code></pre>
        <aside><span>Readable systems</span><strong>Data flows forward.</strong><p>No hidden framework lifecycle. No ambient authority. Each transformation says what it changes.</p></aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Motif kind="aperture" class="closing">
      <div><p class="hara-kicker">Documentation is now part of Hara</p><h2>Learn the language without leaving the site.</h2><a class="button primary" href="/docs/start/orientation/">Read the docs</a></div>
    </Motif>
  </main>
  <script>
    const buttons = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-example]");
    buttons.forEach((button) => button.addEventListener("click", () => {
      buttons.forEach((item) => item.classList.toggle("active", item === button));
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-code]").forEach((code) => { code.hidden = code.dataset.code !== button.dataset.example; });
    }));
    const kernelMode = document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>("#kernel-mode");
    kernelMode?.addEventListener("change", () => document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-kernel-mode]").forEach((panel) => {
      panel.hidden = panel.dataset.kernelMode !== kernelMode.value;
    }));
  </script>
</SiteLayout>
